p/httputil` does this when copying the body fails), so it shouldn't be treated as a crash.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `recovery.go`: the panic value is checked with `errors.Is(e, http.ErrAbortHandler)` first, so wrapped versions are caught too, and then falls through to the existing broken-pipe check.\n- `recovery_test.go`: new test `TestPanicWithAbortHandler`. It checks that the status the handler already set (204) is kept, that the error is logged, and that the \"panic recovered\" stack-trace output doesn't appear.\n\n`gofmt` is clean and `go test ./...` passes.\n\nOne choice to flag: the middleware swallows the panic. It doesn't re-panic and pass it on to `net/http`. Because of that, a response that has already started isn't cut off mid-stream the way the standard library would do it; gin just stops running the remaining handlers. This matches how gin already treats broken pipes.\n\nThe changes aren't committed yet. If you want them committed, I'd use your title, `fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover`. You're on a detached HEAD, so I'd create a branch first.","ttft_ms":2004,"type":"result","duration_ms":26664,"uuid":"a0fae95a-a717-43c8-9a67-59b14e49226d","ttft_stream_ms":1111,"time_to_request_ms":301,"first_content_frame_ms":1111,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
